2 CONT Notes: Family records say he lived at Blumenort and Zeyersvorderkampen in Prussia. Peter may have migrated from Prussia to the Chortitza Colony, S. Russia in either 1793 or 1794 (P. Rempel, p. 9; B. H. Unruh, p. 244, 301). In the 
2 CONC 1795 Chortitza Colony Census he is listed as a Flemish Mennonite (B. H. Unruh, p. 244). It has been speculated that he settled in the village of Chortitza shortly after his arrival (P. Rempel, p. 9). He is listed in the 1797 Chort
2 CONC itza Colony Census, but not in a specific village (P. Rempel, p. 55). In the 1801 Chortitza Colony Census he is listed at Chortitza #38. In the May and September 1813, May 1814, and October 1816 censuses he is listed at Chortitza 
2 CONC #26. In the 1835 Chortitza Colony Census he was listed at Chortitza #18 or 28. His registration was transferred to Schoenthal, Bergthal Colony about 1837 since he and his family are among a group of Mennonites who expressed a desi
2 CONC re to settle in Schoenthal per Odessa Archive, Fond 6, Inventory 1, File 4889.
2 CONT Note: Extracts of the 1850 census for those who moved to Friedrichsthal from other Bergthal villages give his name as Peter Tomas, which is consistent with various speculation that he was the son of Thomas Sawatzky.
2 CONT Note: A history by C. J. (Adeline) Friesen called "Sawatzky 1760 - 1978" documents the Minnesota branch.
2 CONT =======
