2 CONC undert, page 244, 262 Birth Source:  1816 Chortitza Colony Census (October, 1816) Death Source:  Leike Family Records Notes:  He migrated from Plauschwarren, Tilsit region, East Prussia to the Chortitza Colony, S. Russia about 179
2 CONC 3 and settled in Schoenwiese (B. H. Unruh, p. 243, 262; P. Rempel, p. 51). In the 1795 Chortitza Colony Census he is listed at Schoenwiese #11 where his surname is given incorrectly as Lemke in B. H. Unrh's translation of the cens
2 CONC us (B. H. Unruh, p. 244), but his surname is correctly given as Leike in the original document. In the 1797 Census he is listed in Schoenwiese (P. Rempel, p. 51). Between 1797 and 1801 he exchanged homesteads with Martin Wiens #15
2 CONC 916 of Rosenthal (B. H. Unruh p. 252, 253). In the 1801 Chortitza Colony Census he is listed at Rosenthal #34. In the 1808 Census he is listed at Rosenthal #10 (B. H. Unruh, p. 262). In the 1814 Chortitza Colony Heads of Household
2 CONC s List and in the Oct 1816 Census he is listed at Chortitza #18. He is mentioned in Russian passport records as the Beisitzer or assistant to the Oberschultz of the Chortitza Colony beginning in 1813. According to files in the Dne
2 CONC propetrovsk Archives he was involved in a court case during the period 4 Nov 1818 to 13 Aug 1820. The result was that two Jewish men from Cherson were ordered to pay him 2000 rubles for not paying him enough for grain in 1817. He 
2 CONC was a weaver in 1801 and a farmer in 1808 per the 1801 and 1808 Censuses. Family records of the Leike family from Mennonite Genealogy, Inc. of Winnipeg, Manitoba originally gave his o. s. birth date as 26 Aug 1773 and his death da
2 CONC te as 13 Aug 1852. His birth date was later changed in the record to 26 Feb 1773, which seems likely to have been incorrect. His death date was later changed to what appears to be 15 Jan 1852. One record says his gather was Franz 
2 CONC Leyke, born abt 1750. Immigration Source:  Die niederlandisch-niederdeutschen Hintergrunde der mennonitischen Ostwanderungen im 16., 18. und 19. Jahrhundert, page 244 Last update:	5 Oct 2016            This date covers only this i
