2 CONT Francis Bryan MyHeritage Family Trees SCHOENFELD Family Tree in LEWIS JOHNSON Web Site, managed by BARBARA SCHOENFELD (Contact) Birth:	June 1 1630 - County Clare, Munster Province, Ireland Death:	Apr 1 1693 - Belfast, Antrim, Nort
2 CONC hern Ireland Parents:	William “smith” Bryan, Catherine Bryan (born Unknown) Siblings:	Catherine Bryan (born Bryant), Thomas Bryan, Sr, Morgan Bryant, I, John “smith” Bryan, Of Nansemond, James Bryant, William Smith Bryan, Jr., Mar
2 CONC garet Bryant, David Bryant, Sr., <Private> Bryan, 5, <Private> Bryant (Born Bryan), Edmund Bryan, <Private> Bryant, Christian Council (born Bryan) Wife:	Sarah A. Bryan, Of Nansemond (born Brungger Or Brinker) Children:	David Danie
2 CONC l Bryan, Edward Bryan, Lewis Bryan Sr., Joseph Bryan, Brian Bryan, Sir Francis Iv Bryan Iv, Morgan (D. On Easter Sunday Morgan // Morgan Gf Of Rebka /bryan/ Morgan Jr. /bryan/) Bryan, I, Martha Bryan, William Smith Bryan, I, Willi
2 CONC am Smith/ Morgan Bryan, I, Susannah Bryan, Cornelius O. Bryan (Born O'bryan), I, Brian O'bryan, <Private> Bryan, <Private> Unknown, William Bryan
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Traditional
2 CONT
2 CONT “Francis Bryan III , eldest son of William Smith Bryan and Catherine Morgan, was born about 1630 in Virginia. He returned to Ireland in 1667 and tried to regain the Clare County estates, but was persecuted by the government and fo
2 CONC rced to seek refuge in Denmark.  He married Sarah Brinker of Denmark (a cousin to the Princess of Orange) and was an ancestor of Rebecca Bryan  who married Daniel Boone . He was permitted to return to Ireland about 1683, and is sa
2 CONC id to have been standard bearer to William of Orange at the battle of the Boyne. He '''died in Belfast in 1694.''' He had two sons, William, born in Ireland, and Morgan, born in Demark. Both came to America.”
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William Smith Bryan '''died in 1667 in Virginia''' and eldest son, Francis Bryan III, returned to England to reclaim family titles and estates (Cromwell and the Republic were gone and the monarchy under Charles II had been restore
2 CONC d).
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis III got into political difficulties with the Crown and fled to the court of William of Orange in The Hague. There he married Sarah Brinker ca. 1670, a Hollander, and moved to her family's property in Denmark. In late 1671,
2 CONC  Morgan Bryan was born there, as were four or five subsequent children. Charles II of England was succeeded on the throne by James II. This allowed Francis Bryan III to return to England. However, James II also refused to restore 
2 CONC the Bryan family lands and titles to Francis III so he opted to join the movement to replace the unpopular Catholic James II with his Protestant sister Mary Tudor and her husband, William of Orange.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Bryan III and son Morgan, fought in the Battle of Boyne (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_the_Boyne) and at the Siege of Limerick [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Limerick_(1690)] in Ireland with adherents of W
2 CONC illiam of Orange. Although King James II was ousted and he was succeeded by William III and Mary, Francis Bryan III again failed to win Crown support for the recovery of family property. He died in 1694 and was buried in Belfast. 
2 CONC Morgan and several purported siblings subsequently came to America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ms. Hewlett's posting of May 5, 2007, may be a scrambled version of an event described in a family history written in 1830 by a grandson of Morgan and Martha Strode Bryan. - Martha Strode and her two brothers were small children w
2 CONC hen they sailed with their parents for Philadelphia from Holland in company with a group of Huguenots. The parents, both English, were political refugees. Both died en route and those aboard ship arranged to have their children bo
2 CONC und out upon making port. After serving her indenture, Martha was 21 when she married Morgan Bryan ca. 1718.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT These notes are verbatim as published in the Register of Kentucky State Historical Society, Volume 40, No. 132, pp. 318-322. C1974 KY State Historical Society-Frankfort. Edward Bryan, the compiler, is descended from Morgan Bryan. 
2 CONC He was born in Louisville, but at the time of the publication, lived in Colorado.
2 CONT
