1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY: http://www.mhsc.ca/index.asp?cont
2 CONT ent=http://www.mhsc.ca/encyclopedia/contents/T580ME.html
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: BIOGRAPHICAL SKETCH:
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Anna Thiessen was born on January 26, 1892, to Jacob W. Thiessen (1868-1933)
2 CONT and Helena Siemens (1870-1939) in Vassieljewka, Russia as the oldest child.
2 CONT The family emigrated to Manitoba in 1903, and finally settled in Saskatchewan
2 CONT in 1906.  Anna spent two years at the Herbert Bible School from 1913-1915.
2 CONT When she was finished her schooling she was hired by William J. Bestvater who,
2 CONT originally from Mountain Lake, Minnesota, founded a mission in the city of
2 CONT Winnipeg in 1913.  The original six month position included door to door
2 CONT visitations, recruiting children for Sunday school, bringing food and clothing
2 CONT to those in need, visiting the sick, and leading Sunday school.  Her term was
2 CONT extended which became eight years.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: In 1923, she went to school in Los Angeles BIOLA, for two years to improve her
2 CONT English and Biblical knowledge.  Upon her return she was again hired by the
2 CONT city mission, now directed by C.N. Hiebert.  She began to work with immigrants
2 CONT in the city - some of whom were there to find work and repay their immigration
2 CONT debts. Thiessen recognized that these young women needed help in living and
2 CONT work arrangements.  The Canadian Mennonite Brethren Conference saw the validity
2 CONT in Thiessen=s work and provided her with some support.  She worked with this
2 CONT mission until 1925, when she began her time with the Mary Martha Home.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: The Mary Martha Home in Winnipeg was established in an effort to assist
2 CONT Mennonite young women who came from the rural areas to Winnipeg to work in
2 CONT domestic service.  The Mennonite Brethren conference supported Anna Thiessen in
2 CONT 1925, as the matron of the home who provided a home away from home for these
2 CONT young women.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Gatherings were held on Sundays and on afternoons off at the home on Burrows
2 CONT Avenue.  The home experienced a constant flow of traffic of young women coming
2 CONT into the city for work, dropping in on their day off, or perhaps recuperating
2 CONT from illness.  The home founded an association called ATabea Verein@ which
2 CONT focused on giving women a way of meeting with others in the same position, with
2 CONT some of the same concerns.  At its height the home was associated with 150 to
2 CONT 200 women working in Winnipeg.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Anna Thiessen filled the role of a friend, sister, and surrogate mother.  She
2 CONT provided for the spiritual, emotional, and physical well being of the girls.
2 CONT In 1932, the Mary Martha Home was permanently housed in a sixteen room house on
2 CONT Mountain Avenue.  It remained in operation until 1959.  Anna Thiessen died on
2 CONT April 1, 1977.
