1 NAME Peter T. /Funk/
2 GIVN Peter T.
2 SURN Funk
2 _MARNM Funk
2 NICK Thiessen
2 NICK Peter Funk
1 NAME Peter T. //
2 GIVN Peter T.
2 SURN Funk
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Grunthal Cemetery, Gruenthal, East Reserve, Manitoba, Canada
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 29 OCT 1864
2 PLAC Schoenfeld, Bergthal, Ukraine
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 20 FEB 1927
2 PLAC RM of Hanover, Manitoba, Canada
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Family Note: Peter T. Funk and Maria Rempel were 21
2 CONT and 18 years of age respectively when they
2 CONT were married at the Rempel home in
2 CONT Grunthal in 1886. They were second cous ins since Peter’s maternal grandmother,
2 CONT Katharine (Rempel) Thiessen (b.1799) was
2 CONT a sister to Maria’s paternal grandfather, Pe ter Rempel (b. 1807). (See Henry
2 CONT Schapansky, Peter Rempel of Bergthal,
2 CONT Preservings No.8, Pt.I, p. 47)
2 CONT Copies of their “Verlobnis” (betrothal) an nouncement dated July 9, 1886 invited guests
2 CONT to their home for a celebration on the follow ing day, Saturday (July 10) at 10:00 a.m. The
2 CONT Wedding Invitation is signed and dated at
2 CONT Grünthal on July 18, 1886 with the celebra tion taking place on Tuesday the 20th at 1:00
2 CONT p.m.
2 CONT Peter and Maria lived with his parents in
2 CONT Gnadenfeld for the first two years of marriage
2 CONT during which time their oldest child
2 CONT Margaretha was born. They then purchased
2 CONT the east half of NW 2-5-5E in Kronsgart in
2 CONT April 1888 for the amount of $100. Here they
2 CONT built their first house which had four rooms.
2 CONT It was in this little house that 11 more chil dren were born.
2 CONT A new house was built in 1915 surrounded
2 CONT by an extensive white picket fence. This house
2 CONT continued to stand at its original location until
2 CONT 1994 when it was moved to the extreme south
2 CONT end of Barkfield Road. An heirloom that has
2 CONT been preserved for future generations is a
2 CONT painted picture (measuring 27 by 18 inches)
2 CONT of the farmyard that was painted sometime af ter the new house was finished. This picture
2 CONT hung in the Funk living room for many years
2 CONT and is remembered to have been a feature in
2 CONT their home as early as the 1920s.
2 CONT No one knows who the artist was or when
2 CONT it was done. The only markings on the picture
2 CONT are the initials “P.K.” found in the lower left
2 CONT hand corner. This picture in itself is an indica tion of their affluence.
2 CONT In December 1891, Peter bought the
2 CONT northeast quarter of the same section of land from Heinrich Streimer for $250.
2 CONT The R.M. of Hanover assessment
2 CONT roll for 1898 shows Funk as own ing 241 acres, 2 horses, 6 cows,
2 CONT 5 head of cattle under the age of
2 CONT 3 years, 2 sheep and 2 pigs. How ever, the 1910 Assessment Roll
2 CONT shows a significant increase in
2 CONT land holdings and livestock. At
2 CONT that time, he owned 587 acres of
2 CONT land, 6 horses, 1 bull, 12 cows
2 CONT and 4 pigs.
2 CONT The notation of “Farm Imple ment Stock” is also made beside
2 CONT his name with the figure of $200.
2 CONT encircled under the column head ing “Personal Assessment”. On an other line, the figure of $870 also
2 CONT appears under the same heading of
2 CONT “Personal Assessment”. The figure
2 CONT for the “Total Assessable” for this
2 CONT year was $1961, by far the highest
2 CONT amount for that page on the assess ment roll. The next highest was
2 CONT $1200 for neighbour Jacob Mar tens.
2 CONT Municipal Dealings
2 CONT Peter T. Funk was involved in various mu nicipal affairs. Reading through the Council
2 CONT Minutes for the R.M. of Hanover until the year
2 CONT 1910, his name is recorded a number of times
2 CONT for such positions as fire watchman, road boss,
2 CONT and weed inspector.
2 CONT Business.
2 CONT From the one existing ledger that records
2 CONT Funk’s business dealings from 1907 until his
2 CONT death, one can only imagine how much traffic
2 CONT frequented their farmyard. It would seem that
2 CONT Funk had his own hardware store onpremises.
2 CONT Hundreds of entries record the daily transac tions of the time. Equipment that was sold in cluded cultivators, shoe drills ($84.00 in 1908),
2 CONT McCormick binders ($30.00 in 1907), brush
2 CONT breakers ($24.00 in 1909), harrows, a fanning
2 CONT mill ($36.00 in 1909) and even sleighs ($34.00
2 CONT in 1911). Some second-hand equipment was
2 CONT also sold.
2 CONT Apart from being an agent for Massey Har ris Machinery, Funk had a gas pump on his
2 CONT front yard and sold gas to neighbours in the
2 CONT area (25 cents a gallon in 1910 and rising in
2 CONT price to 35 cents a gallon in 1923).
2 CONT Wheelbarrows seemed to be the going item
2 CONT in 1911 when Funk was selling them for $5.75
2 CONT each. Common entries were for such merchan dise as sisal rope, fence wire, cream separator
2 CONT oil, bolts, clips and rivets, guards and shanks,
2 CONT oil cans, coal oil, rake teeth, trees for harness ing horses, and brooms and brushes.
2 CONT In 1911, coffee was a common item that
2 CONT was charged to his clients’ accounts. Priced at
2 CONT approximately 15 cents a pound, some custom ers purchased up to 60 pounds at a time.
2 CONT Entries in 1909-1910 show which custom ers were charged for telephone services as the
2 CONT Funk household operated the “central” from
2 CONT their home.
2 CONT Beginning around 1918, Funk began to take
2 CONT wheat and potatoes as payment on account
2 CONT which he in turn sold to other customers. One
2 CONT entry in 1918 shows the sale of Timothy hay
2 CONT seed for 7 cents a pound. In 1924, one entry
2 CONT records the sale of 221 pounds of flour for
2 CONT $7.65. Wool was another item that he sold in
2 CONT the 1920s for 15 cents a pound.
2 CONT Funk also operated a mill and has recorded
2 CONT the income generated from his mill for the years
2 CONT 1919 to 1926. The last notation for mill income
2 CONT was recorded in 1928 which was the year fol lowing his death.
2 CONT His customer base was mostly local but ex tended not only to the Mennonites but to the
2 CONT French in St.Malo and the Ukrainians as well.
2 CONT Of the 73 names listed in his “index” for his
2 CONT ledger, 56 are Mennonite names and 17 are
2 CONT non-Mennonite. Unfortunately, not all ledger
2 CONT sheets are accounted for as some pages have
2 CONT either fallen out over the years or have been
2 CONT purposely removed
2 CONT Loans.
2 CONT An intriguing discovery in the
2 CONT ledgers was the amount of money
2 CONT that Funk loaned out to his clien tele, not only for credit for pur chases but straight cash loans at an
2 CONT agreed upon interest rate. Ranging
2 CONT anywhere from $1.50 to $500 per
2 CONT loan, it would seem that he was as
2 CONT busy banking as he was
2 CONT storekeeping. Most often no par ticulars were noted for the entries
2 CONT marked “Geld geliessen”, however,
2 CONT on one or two occasions he has
2 CONT written “zum doktor”.
2 CONT Peter T. Funk was not your ste reotypical rural Mennonite of the
2 CONT early 1900s. From his ledger en tries, we can see that he was at least
2 CONT functionally fluent in English. One
2 CONT wonders if he had any knowledge
2 CONT of Russian as he dealt with his
2 CONT Ukrainian neighbours. His finan cial portfolio also included Gov ernment Bonds which he pur chased in 1918 and 1919. One
2 CONT would think that a staunch Mennonite would
2 CONT not have invested in bonds, especially since
2 CONT World War I (and the issue of conscription) had
2 CONT just ended. His ledger heading of “An den
2 CONT Regierung Geld Geliessen” offers more ques tions than answers.
2 CONT This same ledger was used after Funk’s
2 CONT death in 1927. Meticulous records were kept
2 CONT in regards to the salaries his widow paid out to
2 CONT the single children who continued to farm the
2 CONT land. As well, each year’s taxes were recorded.
2 CONT During the early 1930s, Maria Funk continued
2 CONT to loan out some money but on a very limited
2 CONT basis. All entries in this surviving ledger ended
2 CONT in 1937 when Maria died.
2 CONT Business Acumen
2 CONT It almost seems that the business acumen
2 CONT was inherited from the Rempel side of the fam ily seeing as both Peter T. and Maria were de scendants from Johann Rempel (b. 1772). An other daughter of this Johann Rempel was
2 CONT Maria (b.1796) who married Jacob Friesen;
2 CONT their great grandson, David W. Friesen founded
2 CONT the firm“D.W.Friesen & Sons” in Altona.
2 CONT Peter T. Funk’s second cousin and brother in-law, Johann S. Rempel was well-known in
2 CONT the village of Chortitz, E.R. as he operated a
2 CONT small bank there, a branch of the St. Pierre bank
2 CONT as well as being postmaster and serving sev eral terms as municipal secretary in the early
2 CONT 1900s.
2 CONT Funk’s first cousin, Johann Funk Braun, was
2 CONT also an agent for the Massey Harris company
2 CONT in nearby Gnadenfeld. Braun became
2 CONT Grunthal’s first prominent businessman and
2 CONT was involved in a variety of municipal affairs.
2 CONT He was a Commissioner of Oaths and Convey ancer of the Province of Manitoba for many
2 CONT years. (See Preservings No. 8, Pt.I, p. 44)
2 CONT In contrast to Peter’s knack for making a
2 CONT decent living in an area that wasn’t prime agri cultural land, his brother Abram T. Funk of
2 CONT nearby Barkfield struggled financially. How ever, the business acumen can be seen by the
2 CONT success of Abram T.’s grandson, businessman
2 CONT Peter Funk of Funk’s Toyota in Steinbach (see
2 CONT book review, Preservings, No. 10, Part Two,
2 CONT pager 81).
2 CONT Another member of the extended Rempel
2 CONT family who made a noteworthy contribution to
2 CONT the area was Peter Toews (b.1839) who served
2 CONT as Reeve of the R.M. of Hanover, beginning in
2 CONT 1883 (Preservings No. 10, Pt.I, p.69). Toews’
2 CONT mother was Helena Rempel (b. 1809), another
2 CONT daughter of Johann Rempel (b.1772). (Accord ing to historian Henry Schapansky who noted
2 CONT the error on the notation made on BGB A47)
2 CONT Anecdotes.
2 CONT Because Peter T. Funk died in 1927 of what
2 CONT was believed to have been stomach cancer,
2 CONT none of his grandchildren have clear memo ries of him at all since they were very young
2 CONT children at the time of his death. However,
2 CONT some oral history has been preserved and from
2 CONT this we can piece together a bit of the home
2 CONT life as it revolved around the Funk household.
2 CONT Peter is remembered as being a typical pa triarchal head of the household who wasn’t par ticularly talkative in nature. It is said that he
2 CONT was not a man who joked around. Neighbour
2 CONT Jacob Martens was the exact opposite and on
2 CONT one occasion, Martens noticed that the Funks
2 CONT had a chimney fire. Calling up his neighbour,
2 CONT Martens chose a slightly indirect way of alert ing (or irritating) him by telling him the
2 CONT “Funki” (sparks) were flying out of his chim ney. Being unaware of the purpose of this call,
2 CONT Peter retorted, “and at your house the Martens
2 CONT are flying out of the chimney”, (Preservings
2 CONT No. 14, p. 108).
2 CONT The “ackschoap” (corner cupboard) in the
2 CONT living room held his important personal and
2 CONT business papers and was kept under lock and
2 CONT key. He was stern and quite strict in his deal ings with his children and believed that his
2 CONT wife, Maria, was not to do any amount of work
2 CONT around the house and yard - this is what their
2 CONT children were there for.
2 CONT Of their 12 children, six remained single
2 CONT and lived at home, although four of these six
2 CONT married later in life. Chores in and around the
2 CONT house were divided between four single daugh ters. Daughters Mary and Katherine did all
2 CONT the baking, Anne stayed in the house and op erated the “central” telephone exchange from
2 CONT their living room, and Helen took care of the
2 CONT chickens and the gardening. While single son
2 CONT John would haul logs from Marchand to their
2 CONT home in Kronsgart, it was the girls who had to
2 CONT strip the bark so that they could be sharpened
2 CONT for fence posts. Daughter Anne was an expert
2 CONT seamstress and offered her services to others
2 CONT while she was still single.
2 CONT However, it wasn’t always work and no play.
2 CONT Singing played a large part in their leisure time.
2 CONT Daughter Anne played the organ and guitar and
2 CONT son Franz played the violin. Their musical tal ent was inherited from the Rempel side as
2 CONT Maria (Rempel) Funk’s brothers Cornelius and
2 CONT Abram were excellent singers. Her brother
2 CONT Johann is remembered for having introduced
2 CONT the Gesangbuch mit Ziffern (songbook wit
2 CONT numbered notes) into the Chortitzer Church
2 CONT which caused quite a controversy at the time.
2 CONT He also bought musical instruments such as
2 CONT an organ and guitar for his family. (Preservings
2 CONT No. 8, p. 48).
2 CONT References to the Rempel musical talent can
2 CONT be found in a number of articles found in the
2 CONT Preservings, such as Issue No. 15, p. 116 which
2 CONT also features a photo of the Chortitz
2 CONT Orchestra. This same issue also in cludes a fascinating article on the
2 CONT “Ingenious Funk Brothers of
2 CONT Altona” (p. 128) who were also re lated to Maria (Rempel) Funk.
2 CONT These Funks were famous in the
2 CONT area as master builders, mathema ticians, inventors and musicians.
2 CONT The reference to their self-taught
2 CONT musical ability to play a variety of
2 CONT instruments including the cello,
2 CONT guitar and violin leads us then to
2 CONT believe that the “Rempel musical
2 CONT talent” had been inherited from the
2 CONT Sawatske connection as follows:
2 CONT the father to these Funk brothers
2 CONT was Peter S. Funk, a first cousin to
2 CONT Maria (Rempel) Funk. Maria’s
2 CONT mother, Magareta (Sawatske)
2 CONT Rempel was a sister to Katarina
2 CONT Sawatske (married to Peter Funk);
2 CONT they were daughters of Johann
2 CONT Sawatske (b.1804).
2 CONT Grandchildren.
2 CONT Christmas time at the Peter T.
2 CONT Funk’s always meant some sort of
2 CONT present for each grandchild. Some times it was a handkerchief with a
2 CONT coin tied in the corner but one
2 CONT memorable Christmas saw stuffed
2 CONT animals given to the grandchildren.
2 CONT They measured about six inches
2 CONT long and three inches in height and
2 CONT the stuffed cows are remembered
2 CONT as having white and red fabric
2 CONT
2 CONT patches on them. However, unlike some house holds, the Funks didn’t follow the tradition of
2 CONT reciting Christmas verses and wishes so the
2 CONT grandchildren were spared the agony of lining
2 CONT up and waiting their turn.
2 CONT The Funk household was always open to
2 CONT their grandchildren, even if that meant having
2 CONT them live there for several months either for
2 CONT reasons of health or giving some relief to their
2 CONT daughters or daughter-in-laws when there was
2 CONT a new arrival in the family. One thing that most
2 CONT all grandchildren remembered was upon tak ing their leave for home, was their Grandma
2 CONT always calling after them “Kommt Vadda,
2 CONT kommt vadda” (Come again).
2 CONT However, there were certain rules that had
2 CONT to be adhered to if you stayed at
2 CONT the Funks. Cleanliness was ex tremely important and one grand daughter recalled being made to
2 CONT wash her hands before being al lowed to work in the kitchen as she
2 CONT had just changed a diaper. Al though she wasn’t surprised by the
2 CONT request, it was something that
2 CONT wasn’t enforced in most homes.
2 CONT This same granddaughter stayed at
2 CONT her grandparents’ home for some
2 CONT three months while she was in need
2 CONT of the services of chiropractor
2 CONT (Schmedt) Friesen. She constantly
2 CONT fidgeted and had difficulty stand ing still for any length of time.
2 CONT During her stay there, another
2 CONT granddaughter from a different
2 CONT family was also living there. Since
2 CONT these two girls were much the
2 CONT same age, it was expected that the
2 CONT fidgety one would learn by ex ample from the calmer, quieter
2 CONT one.
2 CONT After Peter’s death at the age
2 CONT of 63, his widow continued to live
2 CONT on the farm in Kronsgart until her
2 CONT death some ten years later. Her
2 CONT grandchildren remember her as al ways sitting in her rocking chair.
2 CONT From here she would tell stories
2 CONT to the children. Whereas the Funks
2 CONT tended to be tall and lean, Maria
2 CONT was of generous proportions which
2 CONT was a Rempel characteristic. She
2 CONT seemed to have an endless supply
2 CONT of hard mint candies in her apron pocket that
2 CONT she handed out to her grandchildren.
2 CONT The house and outer buildings on the Funk
2 CONT farmyard were well maintained and painted as
2 CONT was the household furniture. Unlike most of
2 CONT the neighbouring homes, the windows were all
2 CONT hung with curtains. They also had an outbuild ing that was unique in the area. Divided into
2 CONT two rooms, one room contained the traditional
2 CONT two-hole outhouse but the other room was in
2 CONT effect a shower room. It was outfitted with a
2 CONT large pail that hung from the ceiling and had a
2 CONT number of holes in the bottom which served as
2 CONT a sprinkler of sorts. Lowered by a rope, the pail
2 CONT was filled with heated water which was car ried in as needed.
2 CONT It has been incorrectly written (Preservings
2 CONT No. 8, Pt. I, p. 48, Pt. II., p. 63) that Peter T.
2 CONT Funk and his family moved to Paraguay in the
2 CONT 1920s. However, emigration to Paraguay was
2 CONT never an issue and they lived in Kronsgart un til their deaths.
2 CONT As was mentioned, Peter T. Funk died on
2 CONT February 20, 1927 at the age of 63 years. He
2 CONT had undergone surgery (presumably on his
2 CONT stomach) in Winnipeg prior to his death and
2 CONT would sit on the stairway in the kitchen while
2 CONT everyone else ate. Yet he never complained, no
2 CONT matter how ill he felt. Maria Funk suffered a
2 CONT stroke and was paralysed for some time before
2 CONT she passed away on July 24, 1937. Although
2 CONT the Kronsgart cemetery was beside their prop erty, they are both buried in the Grünthal cem etery. This decision had been made prior to
2 CONT their deaths when a badger had unearthed a
2 CONT braid from a recent grave in the Kronsgart cem etery.
2 CONT The funeral invitation written by Peter’s
2 CONT widow Maria, and dated “Kronsgard, Febru ary 20, 1927, is still extant.
2 CONT Other articles as they pertain to the extended
2 CONT Funk family include Al Hamm’s article on
2 CONT “Berliner” Peter K. Funk (Preservings No.
2 CONT 12,p.84) who was a first cousin to Peter T. Funk
2 CONT and “Gnadenfeld - The Funk Clan” by Ernest
2 CONT Braun (Preservings, No. 15, p.90). A photo of
2 CONT a Gesangbuch belonging to a Peter Funk,
2 CONT Bergthal is also found in Issue 15, p. 34. There
2 CONT is speculation that this Peter Funk might in fact
2 CONT be Peter T. Funk but as yet, this has not been
2 CONT verified.
2 CONT Children.
2 CONT 1) Margaretha (1888-1952) married Peter
2 CONT S. Martens, son of neighbour Jacob Martens.
2 CONT Descendants include Margaret Esau of P.V.
2 CONT Esau Insurance, Grunthal; Maggie Smith, wife
2 CONT of lawyer Robert Smith; and John Fehr of
2 CONT Trucks Unlimited.
2 CONT 2) Jacob (1889-1971) married Aganetha
2 CONT Krahn. He was ordained as a minister in the
2 CONT Peter T. Funks’s daughters, l.-r., Maria, Lena, and Tina. They are busy spinning wool. This was usually done
2 CONT during the long winter months so that wool for stockings and sweaters was ready for the next winter. Photo
2 CONT courtesy of Grunthal History, page 66.
2 CONT Chortitzer Church and emigrated to Paraguay
2 CONT in 1948 but returned to Canada in 1959.
2 CONT 3) Agatha (1890-1965) married David F.
2 CONT Wiebe. They left for Paraguay in 1948 but re turned the following year. Descendants include
2 CONT Peter Wiebe, Steinbach Hatchery; Andy Wiebe
2 CONT of Keystone Agri-motive; and Clare Braun,
2 CONT mayor of Niverville.
2 CONT 4) Johan (1893-1978) remained single.
2 CONT 5) Maria (1894-1983) remained single.
2 CONT 6) Peter (1897-1960) married Sara
2 CONT Sawatzky. See Preservings No. 10, Pt. II, p.
2 CONT 40. These were my husband’s maternal grand parents.
2 CONT 7) Franz (1900-86) married Katharina
2 CONT Doerksen. Descendants include Franz Funk of
2 CONT Carleton Hatcheries, Grunthal;
2 CONT 8) Katharina (1902-87) was married at age
2 CONT 35 to Bernhard Friesen, son of “Schmedt”
2 CONT Friesens.
2 CONT 9) Anna (1906-92) married widower John
2 CONT F. Toews in 1948.
2 CONT 10) Aganetha (1908-83) married Jacob
2 CONT Hamm in 1949.
2 CONT 11) Cornelius (1910-83) married Anna
2 CONT Ginter.
2 CONT 12) Helena (1913-91) married widower
2 CONT Jacob Bergen in 1983
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Introduction.
2 CONT For some time now, I have been collecting
2 CONT information on my husband’s maternal greatgrandparents, the Peter T. Funks of Kronsgart,
2 CONT East Reserve. They were known locally as the
2 CONT “Rickje Funke” (rich Funks) as they prospered
2 CONT in what was marginal farm land. However, their
2 CONT prosperity came from entrepreneurial endeavors rather than their farm. Perhaps reading this
2 CONT article will trigger some memories and will
2 CONT spur a descendant to write another article with
2 CONT more anecdotes than I have been able to uncover.
2 CONT Family Background.
2 CONT Peter T. Funk was born on November 10,
2 CONT 1864, the first born child of Jacob Funk (1841-
2 CONT 1906) and Agatha Thiessen (1843-1930) BGB
2 CONT B250. His parents lived in the village of
2 CONT Schönfeld in the Bergthal Colony prior to their
2 CONT emigration to Canada. His paternal grandparents were Peter Funk (1805-66) and Helena
2 CONT Schroeter (1806-69) and his maternal grandparents were Jacob Thiessen (1799-1862) and
2 CONT Katarina Rempel (1799-1875).
2 CONT As one can read by the death dates of both
2 CONT sets of grandparents, Peter T. Funk would only
2 CONT have gotten to know his Thiessen grandmother
2 CONT who died when Peter was almost 11 years old.
2 CONT She accompanied his parents and family on
2 CONT their journey to Canada on the S.S. Quebec,
2 CONT arriving in Quebec on July 20, 1875. However,
2 CONT church records indicate that she died on August 7, 1875, just two weeks after arriving in
2 CONT Canada. It is not known where in Canada she
2 CONT passed away or where she was buried.
2 CONT Peter’s parents, the Jacob Funks settled in
2 CONT Gnadenfeld, E.R. where they farmed on the
2 CONT NW20-5-5. Following Jacob’s death in 1906,
2 CONT his widow Agatha remarried to widower Peter
2 CONT Toews, BGB B273 (see
2 CONT Ernest Braun article for
2 CONT a biography of Jakob S.
2 CONT Funk, Pres., No. 15, page 95). The Toews family is recorded on the 1881 census lists as residing in Bergfeld, E.R. Peter Toews was
2 CONT elected as deacon in the Chortitzer Church in
2 CONT Manitoba in 1885, as minister in 1889 and as
2 CONT Aeltester in 1903.
2 CONT Rempel Family
2 CONT Maria Rempel was born on March 10, 1868
2 CONT to Johann Rempel (1830-99) and Margaretha
2 CONT Sawatzke (1833-1914) BGB B104. The
2 CONT Rempels lived in Heuboden in the Bergthal
2 CONT Colony at the time of Maria’s birth.
2 CONT Maria’s parents along with their 10 children
2 CONT emigrated to Canada arriving in Quebec on July
2 CONT 1, 1875 on the S.S.Moravian. Margaretha
2 CONT Rempel was pregnant on their long and hazardous voyage, giving birth to baby Anna on
2 CONT October 19, not quite four months after their
2 CONT arrival in a new country. Anna, who we assume
2 CONT was born in Ontario, was the youngest child in
2 CONT the family.
2 CONT The Rempel family’s journey was perilous
2 CONT as stormy weather prevailed for most of their
2 CONT journey across the ocean. As they neared the
2 CONT end of their voyage, the captain of the S.S.
2 CONT Moravian lost his bearings on the banks of
2 CONT Newfoundland as they faced yet another storm.
2 CONT When the fog lifted, and they could see how
2 CONT close the ship was to the tall rocky cliffs, they
2 CONT were again thankful and cognizant of God’s
2 CONT tender mercies and His faithfulness.
2 CONT The Rempel family stayed in Ontario during the winter of 1875-76 and were among
2 CONT the 622 people (422 Mennonites with the remainder being French) who boarded the ship
2 CONT “Ontario” in Sarnia and set out for Duluth on
2 CONT May 10, 1876. This trip was interrupted when,
2 CONT on May 16, the ship got stuck in the ice about
2 CONT five miles from their destination. Here they
2 CONT remained for nine days. A small thunderstorm
2 CONT accompanied by strong winds finally freed the
2 CONT ship on the evening of May 24. The Mennonites on board fared quite well as they had
2 CONT brought provisions with them but before the
2 CONT ship was freed from the ice, they had consumed all that they had brought with them,
2 CONT including the seed potatoes and flour which
2 CONT were to get them started as they pioneered in
2 CONT Manitoba.
2 CONT Peter T. Funk and Maria Rempel
2 CONT Peter T. Funk and Maria Rempel were 21
2 CONT and 18 years of age respectively when they
2 CONT were married at the Rempel home in
2 CONT Grunthal in 1886. They were second cousins since Peter’s maternal grandmother,
2 CONT Katharine (Rempel) Thiessen (b.1799) was
2 CONT a sister to Maria’s paternal grandfather, Peter Rempel (b. 1807). (See Henry
2 CONT Schapansky, Peter Rempel of Bergthal,
2 CONT Preservings No.8, Pt.I, p. 47)
2 CONT Copies of their “Verlobnis” (betrothal) announcement dated July 9, 1886 invited guests
2 CONT to their home for a celebration on the following day, Saturday (July 10) at 10:00 a.m. The
2 CONT Wedding Invitation is signed and dated at
2 CONT Grünthal on July 18, 1886 with the celebration taking place on Tuesday the 20th at 1:00
2 CONT p.m.
2 CONT Peter and Maria lived with his parents in
2 CONT Gnadenfeld for the first two years of marriage
2 CONT during which time their oldest child
2 CONT Margaretha was born. They then purchased
2 CONT the east half of NW 2-5-5E in Kronsgart in
2 CONT April 1888 for the amount of $100. Here they
2 CONT built their first house which had four rooms.
2 CONT It was in this little house that 11 more children were born.
2 CONT A new house was built in 1915 surrounded
2 CONT by an extensive white picket fence. This house
2 CONT continued to stand at its original location until
2 CONT 1994 when it was moved to the extreme south
2 CONT end of Barkfield Road. An heirloom that has
2 CONT been preserved for future generations is a
2 CONT painted picture (measuring 27 by 18 inches)
2 CONT of the farmyard that was painted sometime after the new house was finished. This picture
2 CONT hung in the Funk living room for many years
2 CONT and is remembered to have been a feature in
2 CONT their home as early as the 1920s.
2 CONT No one knows who the artist was or when
2 CONT it was done. The only markings on the picture
2 CONT are the initials “P.K.” found in the lower left
2 CONT hand corner. This picture in itself is an indication of their affluence.
2 CONT In December 1891, Peter bought the
2 CONT northeast quarter of the same section of landfrom Heinrich Streimer for $250.
2 CONT The R.M. of Hanover assessment
2 CONT roll for 1898 shows Funk as owning 241 acres, 2 horses, 6 cows,
2 CONT 5 head of cattle under the age of
2 CONT 3 years, 2 sheep and 2 pigs. However, the 1910 Assessment Roll
2 CONT shows a significant increase in
2 CONT land holdings and livestock. At
2 CONT that time, he owned 587 acres of
2 CONT land, 6 horses, 1 bull, 12 cows
2 CONT and 4 pigs.
2 CONT The notation of “Farm Implement Stock” is also made beside
2 CONT his name with the figure of $200.
2 CONT encircled under the column heading “Personal Assessment”. On another line, the figure of $870 also
2 CONT appears under the same heading of
2 CONT “Personal Assessment”. The figure
2 CONT for the “Total Assessable” for this
2 CONT year was $1961, by far the highest
2 CONT amount for that page on the assessment roll. The next highest was
2 CONT $1200 for neighbour Jacob Martens.
2 CONT Municipal Dealings
2 CONT Peter T. Funk was involved in various municipal affairs. Reading through the Council
2 CONT Minutes for the R.M. of Hanover until the year
2 CONT 1910, his name is recorded a number of times
2 CONT for such positions as fire watchman, road boss,
2 CONT and weed inspector.
2 CONT Business.
2 CONT From the one existing ledger that records
2 CONT Funk’s business dealings from 1907 until his
2 CONT death, one can only imagine how much traffic
2 CONT frequented their farmyard. It would seem that
2 CONT Funk had his own hardware store onpremises.
2 CONT Hundreds of entries record the daily transactions of the time. Equipment that was sold included cultivators, shoe drills ($84.00 in 1908),
2 CONT McCormick binders ($30.00 in 1907), brush
2 CONT breakers ($24.00 in 1909), harrows, a fanning
2 CONT mill ($36.00 in 1909) and even sleighs ($34.00
2 CONT in 1911). Some second-hand equipment was
2 CONT also sold.
2 CONT Apart from being an agent for Massey Harris Machinery, Funk had a gas pump on his
2 CONT front yard and sold gas to neighbours in the
2 CONT area (25 cents a gallon in 1910 and rising in
2 CONT price to 35 cents a gallon in 1923).
2 CONT Wheelbarrows seemed to be the going item
2 CONT in 1911 when Funk was selling them for $5.75
2 CONT each. Common entries were for such merchandise as sisal rope, fence wire, cream separator
2 CONT oil, bolts, clips and rivets, guards and shanks,
2 CONT oil cans, coal oil, rake teeth, trees for harnessing horses, and brooms and brushes.
2 CONT In 1911, coffee was a common item that
2 CONT was charged to his clients’ accounts. Priced at
2 CONT approximately 15 cents a pound, some customers purchased up to 60 pounds at a time.
2 CONT Entries in 1909-1910 show which customers were charged for telephone services as the
2 CONT Funk household operated the “central” from
2 CONT their home.
2 CONT Beginning around 1918, Funk began to take
2 CONT wheat and potatoes as payment on account
2 CONT which he in turn sold to other customers. One
2 CONT entry in 1918 shows the sale of Timothy hay
2 CONT seed for 7 cents a pound. In 1924, one entry
2 CONT records the sale of 221 pounds of flour for
2 CONT $7.65. Wool was another item that he sold in
2 CONT the 1920s for 15 cents a pound.
2 CONT Funk also operated a mill and has recorded
2 CONT the income generated from his mill for the years
2 CONT 1919 to 1926. The last notation for mill income
2 CONT was recorded in 1928 which was the year following his death.
2 CONT His customer base was mostly local but extended not only to the Mennonites but to the
2 CONT French in St.Malo and the Ukrainians as well.
2 CONT Of the 73 names listed in his “index” for his
2 CONT ledger, 56 are Mennonite names and 17 are
2 CONT non-Mennonite. Unfortunately, not all ledger
2 CONT sheets are accounted for as some pages have
2 CONT either fallen out over the years or have been
2 CONT purposely removed.
2 CONT Jacob S. Funk and Agatha (Thiessen) Funk, parents of Peter T. Funk. Courtesy of
2 CONT Peter T. Funk Family History, page 3/Preservings, No. 15, page 92.
2 CONT Peter T. Funk and wife Maria (Rempel). Courtesy of
2 CONT Peter T. Funk Family History, page 5.
2 CONT Loans.
2 CONT An intriguing discovery in the
2 CONT ledgers was the amount of money
2 CONT that Funk loaned out to his clientele, not only for credit for purchases but straight cash loans at an
2 CONT agreed upon interest rate. Ranging
2 CONT anywhere from $1.50 to $500 per
2 CONT loan, it would seem that he was as
2 CONT busy banking as he was
2 CONT storekeeping. Most often no particulars were noted for the entriesnearby Barkfield struggled financially. However, the business acumen can be seen by the
2 CONT success of Abram T.’s grandson, businessman
2 CONT Peter Funk of Funk’s Toyota in Steinbach (see
2 CONT book review, Preservings, No. 10, Part Two,
2 CONT pager 81).
2 CONT Another member of the extended Rempel
2 CONT family who made a noteworthy contribution to
2 CONT the area was Peter Toews (b.1839) who served
2 CONT as Reeve of the R.M. of Hanover, beginning in
2 CONT 1883 (Preservings No. 10, Pt.I, p.69). Toews’
2 CONT mother was Helena Rempel (b. 1809), another
2 CONT daughter of Johann Rempel (b.1772). (According to historian Henry Schapansky who noted
2 CONT the error on the notation made on BGB A47)
2 CONT Anecdotes.
2 CONT Because Peter T. Funk died in 1927 of what
2 CONT was believed to have been stomach cancer,
2 CONT none of his grandchildren have clear memories of him at all since they were very young
2 CONT children at the time of his death. However,
2 CONT some oral history has been preserved and from
2 CONT this we can piece together a bit of the home
2 CONT life as it revolved around the Funk household.
2 CONT Peter is remembered as being a typical patriarchal head of the household who wasn’t particularly talkative in nature. It is said that he
2 CONT was not a man who joked around. Neighbour
2 CONT Jacob Martens was the exact opposite and on
2 CONT one occasion, Martens noticed that the Funks
2 CONT had a chimney fire. Calling up his neighbour,
2 CONT Martens chose a slightly indirect way of alerting (or irritating) him by telling him the
2 CONT “Funki” (sparks) were flying out of his chimney. Being unaware of the purpose of this call,
2 CONT Peter retorted, “and at your house the Martens
2 CONT are flying out of the chimney”, (Preservings
2 CONT No. 14, p. 108).
2 CONT The “ackschoap” (corner cupboard) in the
2 CONT living room held his important personal and
2 CONT business papers and was kept under lock and
2 CONT key. He was stern and quite strict in his dealings with his children and believed that his
2 CONT wife, Maria, was not to do any amount of work
2 CONT around the house and yard - this is what their
2 CONT children were there for.
2 CONT Of their 12 children, six remained single
2 CONT and lived at home, although four of these six
2 CONT married later in life. Chores in and around the
2 CONT house were divided between four single daughters. Daughters Mary and Katherine did all
2 CONT the baking, Anne stayed in the house and operated the “central” telephone exchange from
2 CONT their living room, and Helen took care of the
2 CONT chickens and the gardening. While single son
2 CONT John would haul logs from Marchand to their
2 CONT home in Kronsgart, it was the girls who had to
2 CONT strip the bark so that they could be sharpened
2 CONT for fence posts. Daughter Anne was an expert
2 CONT seamstress and offered her services to others
2 CONT while she was still single.
2 CONT However, it wasn’t always work and no play.
2 CONT Singing played a large part in their leisure time.
2 CONT Daughter Anne played the organ and guitar and
2 CONT son Franz played the violin. Their musical talent was inherited from the Rempel side as
2 CONT Maria (Rempel) Funk’s brothers Cornelius and
2 CONT Abram were excellent singers. Her brother
2 CONT Johann is remembered for having introduced
2 CONT the Gesangbuch mit Ziffern (songbook withnumbered notes) into the Chortitzer Church
2 CONT which caused quite a controversy at the time.
2 CONT He also bought musical instruments such as
2 CONT an organ and guitar for his family. (Preservings
2 CONT No. 8, p. 48).
2 CONT References to the Rempel musical talent can
2 CONT be found in a number of articles found in the
2 CONT Preservings, such as Issue No. 15, p. 116 which
2 CONT also features a photo of the Chortitz
2 CONT Orchestra. This same issue also includes a fascinating article on the
2 CONT “Ingenious Funk Brothers of
2 CONT Altona” (p. 128) who were also related to Maria (Rempel) Funk.
2 CONT These Funks were famous in the
2 CONT area as master builders, mathematicians, inventors and musicians.
2 CONT The reference to their self-taught
2 CONT musical ability to play a variety of
2 CONT instruments including the cello,
2 CONT guitar and violin leads us then to
2 CONT believe that the “Rempel musical
2 CONT talent” had been inherited from the
2 CONT Sawatske connection as follows:
2 CONT the father to these Funk brothers
2 CONT was Peter S. Funk, a first cousin to
2 CONT Maria (Rempel) Funk. Maria’s
2 CONT mother, Magareta (Sawatske)
2 CONT Rempel was a sister to Katarina
2 CONT Sawatske (married to Peter Funk);
2 CONT they were daughters of Johann
2 CONT Sawatske (b.1804).
2 CONT Grandchildren.
2 CONT Christmas time at the Peter T.
2 CONT Funk’s always meant some sort of
2 CONT present for each grandchild. Sometimes it was a handkerchief with a
2 CONT coin tied in the corner but one
2 CONT memorable Christmas saw stuffed
2 CONT animals given to the grandchildren.
2 CONT They measured about six inches
2 CONT long and three inches in height and
2 CONT the stuffed cows are remembered
2 CONT as having white and red fabric
2 CONT patches on them. However, unlike some households, the Funks didn’t follow the tradition of
2 CONT reciting Christmas verses and wishes so the
2 CONT grandchildren were spared the agony of lining
2 CONT up and waiting their turn.
2 CONT The Funk household was always open to
2 CONT their grandchildren, even if that meant having
2 CONT them live there for several months either for
2 CONT reasons of health or giving some relief to their
2 CONT daughters or daughter-in-laws when there was
2 CONT a new arrival in the family. One thing that most
2 CONT all grandchildren remembered was upon taking their leave for home, was their Grandma
2 CONT always calling after them “Kommt Vadda,
2 CONT kommt vadda” (Come again).
2 CONT However, there were certain rules that had
2 CONT to be adhered to if you stayed at
2 CONT the Funks. Cleanliness was extremely important and one granddaughter recalled being made to
2 CONT wash her hands before being allowed to work in the kitchen as she
2 CONT had just changed a diaper. Although she wasn’t surprised by the
2 CONT request, it was something that
2 CONT wasn’t enforced in most homes.
2 CONT This same granddaughter stayed at
2 CONT her grandparents’ home for some
2 CONT three months while she was in need
2 CONT of the services of chiropractor
2 CONT (Schmedt) Friesen. She constantly
2 CONT fidgeted and had difficulty standing still for any length of time.
2 CONT During her stay there, another
2 CONT granddaughter from a different
2 CONT family was also living there. Since
2 CONT these two girls were much the
2 CONT same age, it was expected that the
2 CONT fidgety one would learn by example from the calmer, quieter
2 CONT one.
2 CONT After Peter’s death at the age
2 CONT of 63, his widow continued to live
2 CONT on the farm in Kronsgart until her
2 CONT death some ten years later. Her
2 CONT grandchildren remember her as always sitting in her rocking chair.
2 CONT From here she would tell stories
2 CONT to the children. Whereas the Funks
2 CONT tended to be tall and lean, Maria
2 CONT was of generous proportions which
2 CONT was a Rempel characteristic. She
2 CONT seemed to have an endless supply
2 CONT of hard mint candies in her apron pocket that
2 CONT she handed out to her grandchildren.
2 CONT The house and outer buildings on the Funk
2 CONT farmyard were well maintained and painted as
2 CONT was the household furniture. Unlike most of
2 CONT the neighbouring homes, the windows were all
2 CONT hung with curtains. They also had an outbuilding that was unique in the area. Divided into
2 CONT two rooms, one room contained the traditional
2 CONT two-hole outhouse but the other room was in
2 CONT effect a shower room. It was outfitted with a
2 CONT large pail that hung from the ceiling and had a
2 CONT number of holes in the bottom which served as
2 CONT a sprinkler of sorts. Lowered by a rope, the pail
2 CONT was filled with heated water which was carried in as needed.
2 CONT It has been incorrectly written (Preservings
2 CONT No. 8, Pt. I, p. 48, Pt. II., p. 63) that Peter T.
2 CONT Funk and his family moved to Paraguay in the
2 CONT 1920s. However, emigration to Paraguay was
2 CONT never an issue and they lived in Kronsgart until their deaths.
2 CONT As was mentioned, Peter T. Funk died on
2 CONT February 20, 1927 at the age of 63 years. He
2 CONT had undergone surgery (presumably on his
2 CONT stomach) in Winnipeg prior to his death and
2 CONT would sit on the stairway in the kitchen while
2 CONT everyone else ate. Yet he never complained, no
2 CONT matter how ill he felt. Maria Funk suffered a
2 CONT stroke and was paralysed for some time before
2 CONT she passed away on July 24, 1937. Although
2 CONT the Kronsgart cemetery was beside their property, they are both buried in the Grünthal cemetery. This decision had been made prior to
2 CONT their deaths when a badger had unearthed a
2 CONT braid from a recent grave in the Kronsgart cemetery.
2 CONT The funeral invitation written by Peter’s
2 CONT widow Maria, and dated “Kronsgard, February 20, 1927, is still extant.
2 CONT Other articles as they pertain to the extended
2 CONT Funk family include Al Hamm’s article on
2 CONT “Berliner” Peter K. Funk (Preservings No.
2 CONT 12,p.84) who was a first cousin to Peter T. Funk
2 CONT and “Gnadenfeld - The Funk Clan” by Ernest
2 CONT Braun (Preservings, No. 15, p.90). A photo of
2 CONT a Gesangbuch belonging to a Peter Funk,
2 CONT Bergthal is also found in Issue 15, p. 34. There
2 CONT is speculation that this Peter Funk might in fact
2 CONT be Peter T. Funk but as yet, this has not been
2 CONT verified.
2 CONT Children.
2 CONT 1) Margaretha (1888-1952) married Peter
2 CONT S. Martens, son of neighbour Jacob Martens.
2 CONT Descendants include Margaret Esau of P.V.
2 CONT Esau Insurance, Grunthal; Maggie Smith, wife
2 CONT of lawyer Robert Smith; and John Fehr of
2 CONT Trucks Unlimited.
2 CONT 2) Jacob (1889-1971) married Aganetha
2 CONT Krahn. He was ordained as a minister in the
2 CONT Peter T. Funks’s daughters, l.-r., Maria, Lena, and Tina. They are busy spinning wool. This was usually done
2 CONT during the long winter months so that wool for stockings and sweaters was ready for the next winter. Photo
2 CONT courtesy of Grunthal History, page 66.
2 CONT Chortitzer Church and emigrated to Paraguay
2 CONT in 1948 but returned to Canada in 1959.
2 CONT 3) Agatha (1890-1965) married David F.
2 CONT Wiebe. They left for Paraguay in 1948 but returned the following year. Descendants include
2 CONT Peter Wiebe, Steinbach Hatchery; Andy Wiebe
2 CONT of Keystone Agri-motive; and Clare Braun,
2 CONT mayor of Niverville.
2 CONT 4) Johan (1893-1978) remained single.
2 CONT 5) Maria (1894-1983) remained single.
2 CONT 6) Peter (1897-1960) married Sara
2 CONT Sawatzky. See Preservings No. 10, Pt. II, p.
2 CONT 40. These were my husband’s maternal grandparents.
2 CONT 7) Franz (1900-86) married Katharina
2 CONT Doerksen. Descendants include Franz Funk of
2 CONT Carleton Hatcheries, Grunthal;
2 CONT 8) Katharina (1902-87) was married at age
2 CONT 35 to Bernhard Friesen, son of “Schmedt”
2 CONT Friesens.
2 CONT 9) Anna (1906-92) married widower John
2 CONT F. Toews in 1948.
2 CONT 10) Aganetha (1908-83) married Jacob
2 CONT Hamm in 1949.
2 CONT 11) Cornelius (1910-83) married Anna
2 CONT Ginter.
2 CONT 12) Helena (1913-91) married widower
2 CONT Jacob Bergen in 1983.
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Historical Sketches of the East Reserve
2 CONT 1874-1920, p. 165
2 CONT Bergthal Gemeinde Buch, B250, B104
2 CONT Peter T. Funk Family History
2 CONT Jacob Doerksen, “The Jacob Y. Shantz Passenger List,” Preservings, No. 3, p. 5
2 CONT Orlando Hiebert, “Chortitz”, in John Dyck,
2 CONT ed.,Working Papers of the East Reserve Village
2 CONT Histories 1874-1910, p. 45-51.
2 CONT Herman Rempel, Johann S. Rempel 1830-
2 CONT 1899
2 CONT
2 CONT marked “Geld geliessen”, however,
2 CONT on one or two occasions he has
2 CONT written “zum doktor”.
2 CONT Peter T. Funk was not your stereotypical rural Mennonite of the
2 CONT early 1900s. From his ledger entries, we can see that he was at least
2 CONT functionally fluent in English. One
2 CONT wonders if he had any knowledge
2 CONT of Russian as he dealt with his
2 CONT Ukrainian neighbours. His financial portfolio also included Government Bonds which he purchased in 1918 and 1919. One
2 CONT would think that a staunch Mennonite would
2 CONT not have invested in bonds, especially since
2 CONT World War I (and the issue of conscription) had
2 CONT just ended. His ledger heading of “An den
2 CONT Regierung Geld Geliessen” offers more questions than answers.
2 CONT This same ledger was used after Funk’s
2 CONT death in 1927. Meticulous records were kept
2 CONT in regards to the salaries his widow paid out to
2 CONT the single children who continued to farm the
2 CONT land. As well, each year’s taxes were recorded.
2 CONT During the early 1930s, Maria Funk continued
2 CONT to loan out some money but on a very limited
2 CONT basis. All entries in this surviving ledger ended
2 CONT in 1937 when Maria died.
2 CONT Business Acumen
2 CONT It almost seems that the business acumen
2 CONT was inherited from the Rempel side of the family seeing as both Peter T. and Maria were descendants from Johann Rempel (b. 1772). Another daughter of this Johann Rempel was
2 CONT Maria (b.1796) who married Jacob Friesen;
2 CONT their great grandson, David W. Friesen founded
2 CONT the firm“D.W.Friesen & Sons” in Altona.
2 CONT Peter T. Funk’s second cousin and brotherin-law, Johann S. Rempel was well-known in
2 CONT the village of Chortitz, E.R. as he operated a
2 CONT small bank there, a branch of the St. Pierre bank
2 CONT as well as being postmaster and serving several terms as municipal secretary in the early
2 CONT 1900s.
2 CONT Funk’s first cousin, Johann Funk Braun, was
2 CONT also an agent for the Massey Harris company
2 CONT in nearby Gnadenfeld. Braun became
2 CONT Grunthal’s first prominent businessman and
2 CONT was involved in a variety of municipal affairs.
2 CONT He was a Commissioner of Oaths and Conveyancer of the Province of Manitoba for many
2 CONT years. (See Preservings No. 8, Pt.I, p. 44)
2 CONT In contrast to Peter’s knack for making a
2 CONT decent living in an area that wasn’t prime agricultural land, his brother Abram T. Funk of
2 CONT 69 41
2 CONT ever as 81
